  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ecology and Conservation of the Hoolock Gibbon (Hoolock hoolock) in Bangladesh</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Hoolock Gibbons (Hoolock hoolock) are among the most threatened small apes in the world. Because they live in densely populated northeastern India, Bangladesh and western Myanmar, their current status globally is endangered. Population estimates and habitat information are limited for the species. Less than 300 species were estimated to live in scattered forest fragments in Bangladesh in the early 2000s. These numbers have persisted and much needs to be done to allow them to continue. This project proposes to estimate the population size of Hoolock Gibbons in all known areas in Bangladesh, provide detailed habitat quality data and establish the role of Hoolock Gibbons in maintaining forest integrity. Hoolock Gibbon home ranges and movement patterns will be quantified to determine how they allocate time during their activities. Forest Department staff will be trained to collect and manage data. Field guides will be trained to provide small group tours. Public education sessions will be held, with visits to schools and interviews at media outlets to make people aware of the precarious state of the Hoolock Gibbon in Bangladesh and their important role in forest regeneration. Research papers will be published, several reports will be produced to aid the Bangladesh Government to allocate funds to establish protection measures.</narrative>
  </description>
